<article class="env-lemma" id="01TX">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01TX">Lemma <span data-tag="01TX">29.22.9</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $S$ is locally Noetherian and $f$ locally of finite type then $f$ is locally of finite presentation. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $S$ is locally Noetherian and $f$ of finite type then $f$ is of finite presentation. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The first statement follows from the fact that a ring of finite type over a Noetherian ring is of finite presentation, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00FP" data-tag="00FP">10.31.4</a>. Suppose that $f$ is of finite type and $S$ is locally Noetherian. Then $f$ is quasi-compact and locally of finite presentation by (1). Hence it suffices to prove that $f$ is quasi-separated. This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/01T7" data-tag="01T7">29.15.7</a> (and Lemma <a href="/tag/01TW" data-tag="01TW">29.22.8</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>